[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Stacks: LIFO Principle, Implementation (Array, Linked List)"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to Stacks"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"A stack is a fundamental data structure based on the Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) principle. Imagine a stack of plates; you can only add or remove plates from the top. This "top" is the only accessible element in a stack."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"LIFO (Last-In, First-Out) Principle"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"The LIFO principle dictates that the last element added to the stack is the first one to be removed. This behavior is crucial for many applications, such as function call management, expression evaluation, and undo/redo functionality."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"Basic Stack Operations"},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Push: Adds an element to the top of the stack."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Pop: Removes and returns the element at the top of the stack."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Peek (or Top): Returns the element at the top of the stack without removing it."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"isEmpty: Checks if the stack is empty."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"size: Returns the number of elements in the stack."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Stack Implementation using Arrays"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Arrays provide a simple way to implement stacks. We can use an array to store the stack elements and a variable (usually called 'top') to keep track of the index of the top element. When the stack is empty, 'top' is typically initialized to -1."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Array-based Stack Implementation (JavaScript)
class Stack {
  constructor() {
    this.items = [];
    this.top = -1;
  }

  push(element) {
    this.top++;
    this.items[this.top] = element;
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return "Underflow"; // Or throw an exception
    }
    const poppedElement = this.items[this.top];
    this.top--;
    return poppedElement;
  }

  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return "Stack is empty";
    }
    return this.items[this.top];
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.top === -1;
  }

  size() {
    return this.top + 1;
  }
}

// Example Usage
const stack = new Stack();
stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.push(30);
console.log("Top element:", stack.peek()); // Output: 30
console.log("Popped element:", stack.pop()); // Output: 30
console.log("Is stack empty?", stack.isEmpty()); // Output: false
console.log("Stack size:", stack.size()); // Output: 2"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Stack Implementation using Linked Lists"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Linked lists offer a more dynamic approach to stack implementation. Each element in the stack is represented as a node in the linked list. The 'top' of the stack is the head of the linked list. This avoids the fixed-size limitation of arrays."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Linked List-based Stack Implementation (JavaScript)
class Node {
  constructor(data) {
    this.data = data;
    this.next = null;
  }
}

class Stack {
  constructor() {
    this.top = null;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  push(data) {
    const newNode = new Node(data);
    newNode.next = this.top;
    this.top = newNode;
    this.size++;
  }

  pop() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return "Underflow"; // Or throw an exception
    }
    const poppedNode = this.top;
    this.top = this.top.next;
    poppedNode.next = null; // Remove reference
    this.size--;
    return poppedNode.data;
  }

  peek() {
    if (this.isEmpty()) {
      return "Stack is empty";
    }
    return this.top.data;
  }

  isEmpty() {
    return this.top === null;
  }

  getSize() {
    return this.size;
  }
}

// Example Usage
const stack = new Stack();
stack.push(10);
stack.push(20);
stack.push(30);
console.log("Top element:", stack.peek()); // Output: 30
console.log("Popped element:", stack.pop()); // Output: 30
console.log("Is stack empty?", stack.isEmpty()); // Output: false
console.log("Stack size:", stack.getSize()); // Output: 2"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Comparison: Array vs. Linked List Implementation"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Array: Simpler to implement, but has a fixed size (unless dynamically resized, which can be costly). Accessing elements is faster (O(1))."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Linked List: More flexible in terms of size, but requires more memory due to node structure. Accessing elements is slower (O(n) in the worst case, but push/pop are O(1))."},{"order":21,"type":"subheading","text":"Applications of Stacks"},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Function call stack: Managing function calls and returns in programming languages."},{"order":23,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Expression evaluation: Converting infix expressions to postfix or prefix and evaluating them."},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Undo/redo functionality: Storing the history of actions for undoing and redoing."},{"order":25,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Backtracking algorithms: Exploring different possibilities in a search space (e.g., solving mazes)."},{"order":26,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Browser history: Keeping track of visited web pages."}]
